Required Software:
    cmake
    clang
    gcc
    llvm-libs
    lscpu (optional)

Algorithm specification contains the algorithm specification paper and the signed electronic material.
Implementations folder contains the Reference and Optimisations implementations (Performance and Resource optimization versions)
ngcc_bench contains the test bench source that measures the algorithm .so library file
self-assesment folder contains all the conducted tests folders with naming i.e "Digital Signature-Facto-DSA-128-x86-64-Performance Optimization Version". A main report.pdf is included explaining the global results.

run-bench.sh compiles all the implementations into .so library files and invokes ngcc_bench to create results into "Digital Signature-Facto-DSA-...Implementation Name" folders inside "reports" folder.
clean-folders.sh eliminates intermediate compiling sources like .o files to leave the implementation folders clean.

All the implementation sources are platform aware, they adjust to the compiling platform by detecting the architecture in Makefiles.
This is, if you compile in x86 then the implementations will compile in that platform, same for ARM and RISCV

KAT vectors are included in Test_Vectors folders. To generate KAT vectors (Test_Vectors) take any implementation, for example Reference Implementation.
Then do the following 

cd "Implementations/Reference Implementation"
cd "Facto-DSA-128"
make clean && make kat_sig
./kat_sig

(or just make run and you are done!)
The KAT vector result in this case, for 128, is left inside the "output" subfolder. 
To obtain the rest KAT files, do the same for Facto-DSA-256 and Facto-DSA-512 folders.

To reproduce self-assesment data, you can just run the script ./run-bench.sh
Note that run-bench.sh sets CC variable to CC=clang, you can switch in the 1st line to CC=gcc if you want to
